Solution. Treating \((X, Y)\) as a single discrete random object taking one of the four values \((0,0)\), \((0,1)\), \((1,0)\), \((1,1)\), LOTUS (Theorem 3) applied to \(g(x, y) = x + y\) gives directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}
&= \sum_{x \in \{0,1\}} \sum_{y \in \{0,1\}} (x + y)\,\operatorname{P}(X = x,\, Y = y) && \text{(LOTUS)} \\
&= (0{+}0)(0.2) + (0{+}1)(0.3) + (1{+}0)(0.1) + (1{+}1)(0.4) && \text{(expand the four terms)} \\
&= 0 + 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.8 && \text{(multiply)} \\
&= 1.2 && \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
As a check: \(\operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{} = 0(0.5) + 1(0.5) = 0.5\) and \(\operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{} = 0(0.3) + 1(0.7) = 0.7\), so \(\operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{} = \operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{} + \operatorname{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{} = 1.2\) by linearity of expectation.

